Here is the comprehensive design package for a motion‑activated spray device that an intern can build and demo within two weeks.

## 1 Executive Summary

A compact motion‑activated spray device was engineered using mostly off‑the‑shelf parts. An ESP8266 D1 Mini microcontroller monitors a PIR sensor for motion and a force‑sensitive resistor (FSR) for weight detection. Upon detecting movement or pressure, it triggers a small micro‑servo that depresses the nozzle of a 30 mL spray bottle for a short burst, with a built‑in 3‑s lockout to prevent repeated spraying. Power comes from a 3.7 V Li‑ion battery; a Micro‑LiPo charger keeps the cell topped up via USB‑C, and a PowerBoost board generates 5 V for the servo and sensor. A custom 3‑D‑printed enclosure keeps everything compact (120 × 80 × 50 mm) and includes screw bosses, magnet pockets and a PIR lens window. Firmware handles auto‑calibration of the FSR, threshold storage in EEPROM, lockout timing, and LED status indications. Total prototype cost is well below US \$90 (Aug 2025 prices).

